{
  "reviews": [
    {
      "id": "R1",
      "criteria": {
        "credible_improvement_required": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 4,
          "rationale": "A concrete baseline delta is now supported: code:h200:draft-dp-sync contains \"num_batch_tokens = target_dp_sync.num_reqs * num_query_per_req\". The patch separately preserves unpadded counts. Transfer inference: fewer rows can reduce drafting work when graph selection changes. The static capture-size comparator is appropriate. Actual opportunity frequency and exposed H200 cost remain unknown; measuring them is a valid first falsification step."
        },
        "h200_applicability_and_correctness_required": {
          "verdict": "unknown",
          "score": 3,
          "rationale": "The Python execution-shape port is plausible on SM90, and the proposed dummy-rank, graph, KV and sampler checks are well scoped. However, code:h200:port-checks records \"patch does not apply\" in both dp_utils.py and speculator.py. Complete final patch behavior and deployed dispatch remain unverified. This requires adaptation, not an architecture-incompatibility rejection."
        },
        "discriminating_experiment": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 4,
          "rationale": "The design distinguishes logical counts from executed rows, includes eligible-opportunity denominators, controls graph refinement and acceptance differences, and separates mechanism diagnostics from unprofiled serving comparisons. It preserves primary workloads and specifies five alternating paired confirmation blocks. Minimum opportunity coverage and numerical benefit/nonregression thresholds still need preregistration."
        },
        "traceable_engineering_support": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 4,
          "rationale": "Observed supplied artifacts establish the installed helper and portions of the proposed fix. Author-reported H20 DP8, seq96, MBT2048, adaptive-ON random 3500/1500 results support the stated figures; \"Acceptance varies between the two arms\" limits causal attribution. H200 gains remain explicitly predictive. The card should incorporate the additional installed-helper and failed-application artifacts instead of retaining stale uncertainty about helper behavior."
        },
        "practical_value_and_portfolio_coverage": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 4,
          "rationale": "Cheap source and graph-opportunity checks can stop an unproductive port. Ragged traffic provides complementary coverage, while aligned full batches may offer little benefit. Capture-memory costs and parameterized node cost are handled appropriately. Port effort is less certain than the four-file change count suggests."
        }
      },
      "factual_support": "pass",
      "objections": [
        "The supplied installed helper already resolves one claimed evidence gap; update the baseline description.",
        "The four-commit patch fails application checks, so its complete semantic adaptation needs review.",
        "H20 results used separate nodes and changed acceptance; neither reported throughput gains nor GSM8K nonsignificance establishes execution-only benefit or sampler correctness."
      ],
      "required_revision": "Weighted score: 76/100; deferred on applicability. Cite the installed helper and failed application checks, specify the complete port and checkpoint query layout, and preregister coverage and guardrail thresholds. Retain source/activation inspection as the first step; no measured speedup is required to justify that investigation."
    },
    {
      "id": "R2",
      "criteria": {
        "credible_improvement_required": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 4,
          "rationale": "The installed excerpt contains \"dist.all_reduce(tensor, group=group)\", and the supplied patch provides a concrete replacement. Reducing exposed small-message synchronization is a credible mechanism. Matched CPU-placement tuning is a useful simple comparator. Existing-call eligibility and exposed cost can be determined before implementation."
        },
        "h200_applicability_and_correctness_required": {
          "verdict": "unknown",
          "score": 3,
          "rationale": "The CPU algorithm has a plausible DP4 path independent of GPU arithmetic. Nevertheless, actual process-group backend and invocation ordering are unknown, and code:h200:port-checks records a failed parallel_state.py hunk. The patch uses fixed reserved tags; scoped ordering, group-local rank, pause and fallback checks are necessary. The source says \"ray backend not tested\"."
        },
        "discriminating_experiment": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 4,
          "rationale": "Separating arrival skew from last-arrival-to-completion time directly tests the mechanism. Worker and engine-state arms, placement controls, simultaneous replicas, all-rank activation counts and paired held-out confirmation provide strong discrimination. Prespecify timestamp alignment accuracy, acceptable instrumentation overhead and minimum exposed-time coverage."
        },
        "traceable_engineering_support": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 4,
          "rationale": "Observed patch text includes recursive exchanges and \"recv_work.wait()\"; the installed excerpt establishes a relevant call. The 780-to-224-microsecond result is accurately attributed to a four-rank GB200/Ethernet microbenchmark, not serving. Source CPU details, H200 host transport behavior and serving effect remain unknown. The card omits the supplied application failure."
        },
        "practical_value_and_portfolio_coverage": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 4,
          "rationale": "This targets a distinct host synchronization cost without changing MoE communication or GPU precision. Inspection and small-message diagnostics offer inexpensive falsification. Distributed validation and ordering safeguards may outweigh savings, especially if late ranks dominate; the proposal explicitly tests those alternatives."
        }
      },
      "factual_support": "pass",
      "objections": [
        "A generic dist.all_reduce call does not establish the actual Gloo backend or every proposed caller's eligibility.",
        "The supplied patch does not apply cleanly; include that integration evidence.",
        "Two exchange rounds at DP4 describe algorithm structure, not latency or serving improvement.",
        "Ensure the adapted caller enforces CPU SUM/backend eligibility and prevents overlapping invocations from reusing reserved tags ambiguously."
      ],
      "required_revision": "Weighted score: 76/100; deferred on applicability. Add the failed port check, identify eligible process groups and callers, and specify tag/order safeguards and fallback behavior. Preserve separate worker and engine-state attribution and the matched placement comparator."
    },
    {
      "id": "R3",
      "criteria": {
        "credible_improvement_required": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 4,
          "rationale": "The source identifies process-local fragmentation: \"each frontend process has its own client and affinity map\". Shared visibility could recover retained prefix work across API2 workers. The explicit source/routing inspection and final-rank join provide a cheap eligibility check. Stateless hashing and local hints are strong simple comparators."
        },
        "h200_applicability_and_correctness_required": {
          "verdict": "unknown",
          "score": 3,
          "rationale": "A host routing adaptation is plausible on SM90, with appropriate cache isolation, stale-entry, concurrency and bypass checks. The shared-table integration, installed routing behavior and exact prefix-key semantics remain unspecified or unverified. The source's local tie-break implementation does not establish the proposed shared implementation."
        },
        "discriminating_experiment": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 4,
          "rationale": "Final-destination joins, retained-KV observations, avoided prefill tokens and queue-tail measurements distinguish locality gains from queue concentration. Cold/warm and cache-isolated controls are appropriate, and confirmation uses full-run pairs. Refine the opportunity denominator to require that a reusable destination is actually among the tied minimum-score ranks."
        },
        "traceable_engineering_support": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 4,
          "rationale": "PR #52464 supplies a concrete engineering account, named tests and attributed H100 DP4/API4 high-reuse Mooncake results. Its restriction \"only when scores tie\" supports the narrowed intervention. The negative 23% figure is per-user output token rate, which should be labeled precisely. CacheRoute's supplied conclusion supports the negative-workload boundary, not implementation of shared routing."
        },
        "practical_value_and_portfolio_coverage": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 4,
          "rationale": "Native 8K prefixes make this more directly relevant to the primary workload than a cache-isolated pilot. The proposal charges shared-state overhead and rejects hit-rate improvements without serving value. Benefits remain conditional on retained exact-prefix work and within-replica traffic; cross-replica recovery is outside this intervention."
        }
      },
      "factual_support": "pass",
      "objections": [
        "A remembered destination is neither current residency evidence nor proof that the destination remains eligible under load precedence.",
        "Define prefix length and matching across growing conversation turns; a common first block can concentrate unrelated requests.",
        "The author's variance explanation for negative results is unverified.",
        "The shared-state implementation is a transfer proposal, not an implementation demonstrated by the supplied PR."
      ],
      "required_revision": "Weighted score: 76/100; deferred on applicability. Specify prefix-key and update semantics, shared-state synchronization and failure fallback, and the eligible-resident-tied opportunity denominator. Label source throughput as per-user output token rate and retain both simple comparators."
    },
    {
      "id": "R4",
      "criteria": {
        "credible_improvement_required": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 4,
          "rationale": "Coordinating prefills can plausibly reduce repeated lockstep decode interruption. The proposal first checks existing alignment and compares static prefill intervals plus separately labeled MBT tuning, satisfying the baseline-relative mechanism requirement. Unmeasured mixed-step exposure is an appropriate first falsification question."
        },
        "h200_applicability_and_correctness_required": {
          "verdict": "unknown",
          "score": 3,
          "rationale": "The host protocol is plausibly adaptable to fixed DP4/EP4, but installed scheduler compatibility and the proposed overdue mechanism are unverified. The source excludes elastic EP and custom schedulers. A wall-clock flag checked once per step can override HOLD decisions; it does not by itself establish a hard request-wait bound during long steps, repeated chunks or resource starvation."
        },
        "discriminating_experiment": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 4,
          "rationale": "Static-policy controls, fixed offered schedules, unfinished-request accounting, collective overhead and release-reason counters directly address competing explanations. Held-out long chunks and idle-to-active waves are useful. The design appropriately treats an unresolved TTFT tradeoff as inconclusive. Specify request-versus-chunk age, deadline-check cadence and attainable wait guarantees before qualification."
        },
        "traceable_engineering_support": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 3,
          "rationale": "The engineering PR describes the six-int FIRE/HOLD protocol and reports \"No GPU runs on this branch yet.\" Earlier results are author reports from DeepSeek-V4-Pro on MI355X DP8, not this Flash/H200 deployment; at concurrency 64, TTFT rose from 3.76 to 8.63 seconds for little total-throughput change. The supplied packet lacks the implementation diff, and #51199's benchmark hardware is unknown."
        },
        "practical_value_and_portfolio_coverage": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 3,
          "rationale": "Mixed agentic traffic is relevant, and screening static settings first limits wasted integration effort. This remains the most complex scheduler intervention, with substantial TTFT risk and no supplied production tradeoff. Its value is conditional research value, not established deployment suitability."
        }
      },
      "factual_support": "pass",
      "objections": [
        "Name DeepSeek-V4-Pro explicitly as the source model; the reported results do not measure V4.1-Flash.",
        "The wall-clock escape is a new adaptation whose progress semantics need specification.",
        "Earlier-branch results, cross-node comparisons and CPU simulations cannot establish the submitted branch's serving behavior.",
        "The treatment refers to h200-systems-002 rather than the current R2 identifier."
      ],
      "required_revision": "Weighted score: 72/100; deferred on applicability. Define the overdue protocol and realistic wait bound, obtain implementation-level branch support, and preregister development-only tuning and TTFT limits. Correct the source-model attribution and R2 reference. Stop if static tuning dominates."
    },
    {
      "id": "R5",
      "criteria": {
        "credible_improvement_required": {
          "verdict": "fail",
          "score": 1,
          "rationale": "The supplied code:h200:resolved-engram logs resolve \"dp_shared_memory=True, use_thp=False\" on both replicas. R5 explicitly targets rounded private tables and rejects shared storage. Its own cheap eligibility check therefore rules out the proposed delta as scoped. No change to the active shared-storage path is proposed."
        },
        "h200_applicability_and_correctness_required": {
          "verdict": "fail",
          "score": 1,
          "rationale": "Generic CUDA host registration is plausibly portable, but the proposed private-table intervention does not match the resolved shared-storage deployment. R5 preserves ownership and explicitly excludes the shared-backing alternative, leaving no remedy within this proposal. This is a deployment-path mismatch, not evidence that H200 cannot register host memory."
        },
        "discriminating_experiment": {
          "verdict": "pass",
          "score": 4,
          "rationale": "The attribution controls correctly separate exact sizing, registration type, sorting and placement, and the rejection rules explicitly cover shared tables. The experiment is discriminating because the supplied configuration evidence already triggers its stop condition. A later shared-storage proposal would need a different activation denominator and comparison."
        },
        "traceable_engineering_support": {
          "verdict": "fail",
          "score": 2,
          "rationale": "The GB10 TP4/32K lookup figures and \"Exact size gives the memory saving; the registered memory type gives the speed\" are accurately attributed author claims. However, describing shared-versus-private configuration as unresolved ignores the supplied resolved logs. Actual backing, physical bytes and H200 lookup effects remain unknown; those uncertainties do not restore private-path eligibility."
        },
        "practical_value_and_portfolio_coverage": {
          "verdict": "fail",
          "score": 1,
          "rationale": "An ineligible private allocator port should not occupy one of five practical baseline improvements. Its inspection has useful exclusion value, but the proposed serving change has no identified active allocation target. Host savings would not automatically imply GPU KV savings or reduced node cost even in an eligible deployment."
        }
      },
      "factual_support": "fail",
      "objections": [
        "Both replicas resolve shared DP Engram storage; the draft's central deployment uncertainty is stale.",
        "The proposal's own shared-storage rejection condition is met.",
        "GB10 private-table timings do not establish behavior of the deployed shared-storage path.",
        "Resolved shared mode does not establish whether the physical backing is /dev/shm or cuMem, its page mapping, or exposed lookup cost."
      ],
      "required_revision": "Weighted score: 37/100; vetoed as scoped. Record the private-allocation candidate as ineligible and replace it. Any shared-storage investigation must be a separately scoped hypothesis with backing/capability checks, a supported simple comparator and exposed-time measurements; #62359's GB200 results are not H200 predictions."
    }
  ],
  "global_gaps": [
    "R1–R4 remain deferred on applicability; R5 fails required gates. Weighted totals above 70 do not override unknown or failed required gates. No H200 speedup measurement is required to justify the proposed inspection and falsification steps.",
    "The supplied artifacts supersede some draft uncertainty: installed draft sizing is visible, R1/R2 patch application fails, and both replicas resolve shared Engram storage. Incorporate these observations without treating textual patch failure as proof that a port is impossible.",
    "G01–G05 definitions are absent. The cards provide useful scoped tests, but exact contract mapping remains unknown. Speculative acceptance and nonsignificant task-score differences do not establish sampler correctness.",
    "Before future experiments, specify minimum eligible coverage, primary benefit metrics, numerical nonregression thresholds, counter-reset/missing-data handling and TPOT handling for outputs shorter than two tokens. Preserve the planned five alternating paired full-run confirmations and exclude profiled results from the frontier.",
    "H200 effect sizes, measured bottlenecks, full-node price and production SLO remain unknown. Use parameterized eight-GPU cost and distinguish provisional diagnostic goodput from production-qualified value.",
    "The five mechanisms provide complementary systems coverage, but R5's exclusion leaves fewer than five viable candidates. DeepEP alternatives and packed Engram exchange need an explicit disposition; shared-table configuration and architecture/dependency restrictions prevent treating them as automatic replacements.",
    "The brief says 13 repositories but lists 16. The packet includes 64 of 228 available documents; title-only leads and omitted records do not establish engineering coverage or absence of alternatives. BASS methods are absent, and the supplied CacheRoute conclusion supports only attributed context and limitations. Historical rankings and novelty do not determine these verdicts."
  ]
}
